  "text": "ьте\n.first.step\nTo find the points of intersection between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the function \\(f(x)\\), we need to substitute \\(x\\) from the parabola equation into the function definition and solve for \\(y\\). Since \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise, we will consider each piece separately.\n\n.step\n1. **For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):**\n   \\[\n   f(x) = x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 34y^2\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = 34y^2 \\implies 34y^2 = x \\implies y^2 = \\frac{x}{34}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   -1 \\leq x < 1 \\implies -1 \\leq \\frac{x}{34} < \\frac{1}{34}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\leq y < \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(y\\) into the parabola equation:\n   \\[\n   x = 34\\left(\\frac{x}{34}\\right)^2 = 34 \\cdot \\frac{x^2}{1156} = \\frac{x^2}{34}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x = 34y^2\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{(34y^2)^2}{34} = 34y^4\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   x = 34y^4\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x = 34y^2\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   34y^2 = 34y^4 \\implies y^2(1 - y^2) = 0\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   y^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y^2 = 1\n   \\]\n   Since \\(y \\in [-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}})\\), we have \\(y = 0\\) as the only valid solution.\n\n2. **For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):**\n   \\[\n   f(x) = 2 - x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 34y^2\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = 34y^2 \\implies 2 - x = 2 - 34y^2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   1 \\leq x < 3 \\implies 1 \\leq 34y^2 < 3\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 < \\frac{3}{34}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(y\\) into the parabola equation:\n   \\[\n   x = 34y^2 \\implies 2 - x = 2 - 34y^2\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2 - x = 2 - 34y^2 \\implies x = 34y^2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x = 34y^2\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   2 - 34y^2 = 2 - 34y^2\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2 - x = 2 - 34y^2 \\implies x = 34y^2\n   \\]\n   This is consistent with our previous solution.\n\nFinally, we need to sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the intersection points. We have \\(y = 0\\) as the only valid solution for both pieces of the function. Therefore, the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
